ral"},{"slug":"training-to-failure-proximity","title":"Training to Failure: How Close Is Close Enough?","excerpt":"Failure can be a useful calibration point, but current evidence does not show that every set must reach it for muscle growth or strength.","date":"","content":"\n\nRepetitions in reserve (RIR) is an estimate of how many technically sound repetitions remain at the end of a set. Zero RIR means no further repetition could be completed with the chosen standard; tw","category":"General"},{"slug":"training-volume-sets-muscle-growth-2026","title":"Training Volume for Muscle Growth: How to Choose Weekly Sets","excerpt":"More weekly resistance-training volume can support more hypertrophy with diminishing returns, but research does not identify one optimal set count for every muscle or lifter.","date":"","content":"\n\nTraining volume is usually discussed as hard sets per muscle per week. That shorthand is useful, but it hides several decisions: what counts as a set, how close it is to failure, which exercise and ","category":"General"},{"slug":"optimal-set-volume-2025-research","title":"Training Volume Per Session: Why There Is No Universal 16-Set Limit","excerpt":"A recent physique-athlete study raises useful questions about session volume, but it does not establish a 16-set ceiling. Here is how to manage volume from performance and recovery.","date":"","content":"\n“More volume” is not a complete training plan, but neither is a single number a universal limit. The productive amount of work depends on the muscle, exercise, load, effort, training age, frequency, ","category":"General"},{"slug":"training-while-sick-when-to-push-rest","title":"Training While Sick: When to Rest and How to Return","excerpt":"The neck-check rule is only a rough heuristic. Use symptoms, illness severity, contagion, and cardiac warning signs to decide when lifting should pause.","date":"","content":"A runny nose and a fever are not the same training decision. Nor is a mild cold the sam